# Zone X Stores — E-Commerce Order Analysis

**Prepared by Daniel C. Diala** | Portfolio Project | Excel (Pivot Tables, Dashboard)

## Overview

This project analyzes 1,200 e-commerce orders for "Zone X Stores" between January 2023 and June 2025, covering revenue, product performance, payment behavior, order fulfillment, and customer acquisition channels. It follows a full analytics workflow: a raw order export → a light cleaning pass → pivot-table analysis → a KPI dashboard → a written executive summary with recommendations.

**Business question:** Is the business healthy and growing, and if performance is slipping, is it a fulfillment problem, a marketing problem, or simply an incomplete year being compared unfairly to a full one?

---

## 1. Data Cleaning

The raw export was preserved untouched in an "Origin Data" sheet as an audit trail; all cleaning happened in a separate "Clean Data" sheet. The dataset needed relatively little cleaning — only one column had missing values — but a couple of important interpretation issues surfaced while reviewing the workbook and are documented below rather than silently carried forward.

| Issue Found | Detail | How It Was Handled |
|---|---|---|
| Missing CouponCode (309 of 1,200 rows, ~25.8%) | Left blank in the raw export whenever no coupon was used | Relabeled `N/P` (Not Provided) instead of left blank — "no coupon used" is itself meaningful for coupon-usage analysis, so it needed its own visible category rather than disappearing as a null |
| Duplicate OrderIDs | Checked all 1,200 order records | Verified unique — no action needed |
| TotalPrice formula integrity | Checked Quantity × UnitPrice against TotalPrice on every row | Matches exactly on all 1,200 rows — confirms the field can be trusted as-is |
| ShippingAddress lacks real location variety | Every one of the 1,200 addresses uses the same street name ("Main St") — only the house number differs | Left as-is, but flagged: despite looking like a location field, it cannot support city/region-level analysis |
| 2025 is a partial year | Date range runs 01/01/2023–30/06/2025 — 2023 and 2024 each have all 12 months, 2025 has only January–June | Not a data error, but flagged prominently because it changes how the year-over-year revenue figures should be read (see Section 2) |

