## 2. Exploratory Data Analysis

**Headline KPIs** (calculated across the full 1,200-row Clean Data table):

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Total Orders | 1,200 |
| Total Revenue | $1,264,761.96 |
| Total Quantity Sold | 3,535 units |
| Average Order Value | $1,053.97 |
| Unique Customers | 1,189 |

**Key finding — the partial-year trap:** Read at face value, revenue by year looks like a steep decline: 2023 = $552,643.24, 2024 = $480,235.87, 2025 = $231,882.85 — a 13.1% drop followed by what looks like a 51.7% collapse. But 2025 only has 6 months of data. On a fair monthly-average basis: 2023 averaged ~$46,053/month, 2024 averaged ~$40,020/month (-13.1%, a real decline), and 2025 is averaging ~$38,647/month so far — only about 3.4% below 2024's run rate, not over 50%. The 2023→2024 decline is real; the apparent 2024→2025 "collapse" is largely a partial-year artifact.

**Order fulfillment is evenly distributed, not dominated by one status:** Cancelled 250, Returned 247, Pending 237, Shipped 235, Delivered 231 — roughly 19–21% each. The 20.8% cancellation rate is worth monitoring but is not the majority-of-orders problem it might first appear to be. (Note: the workbook's own Summary sheet states 750 cancelled vs. 731 delivered — this analysis uses the verified pivot-table counts instead; see Data Cleaning notes on why.)

**Payment methods are fairly balanced:** Credit Card ($263,847.63) and Online ($262,442.94) lead, followed by Cash ($259,786.29) and Gift Card ($246,323.92); Debit Card is lowest ($232,361.18). The gap between top and bottom is under 12% — a mild skew, not a dramatic one.

**No single product dominates:** Chair ($195,620.11) and Printer ($195,612.61) lead, essentially tied, followed by Laptop ($192,126.56) and Tablet ($186,568.95); Phone is lowest ($151,722.39). A healthier position than a top-heavy product mix.

